<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luis de Góngora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luis de Góngora was a Spanish poet of the Golden Age from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. His Soledades and Fable of Polyphemus and Galatea, with their Latinate syntax, learned allusions and dense metaphors, created the style called culteranismo or Gongorism, which provoked fierce controversy, notably with Quevedo. Long dismissed, he was rediscovered in 1927 by the poets of the Generation of '27, named after his tercentenary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-de-gongora.jpg" alt="Luis de Góngora" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Velázquez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_Rodr%C3%ADguez_de_Silva_y_Vel%C3%A1zquez_-_Luis_de_G%C3%B3ngora_y_Argote_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Luis de Góngora was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 11 July 1561 to a noble family; his father, Francisco de Argote, was corregidor, or judge. At a time when purity of Christian lineage was needed for education or official appointments, he took the surname of his mother, Leonor de Góngora. His uncle, a prebendary of Córdoba Cathedral, renounced his post in favour of his nephew, who took deacon's orders in 1586. As a canon of the cathedral Góngora travelled on commissions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Around 1605 he was ordained priest and afterwards lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. In 1617, through the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma">Duke of Lerma</a>, he was appointed honorary chaplain to King Philip III, but did not enjoy the honour long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Góngora did not publish his poems, though he tried in 1623, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copies circulated in songbooks and anthologies. His two best-known works are the Fábula de Polifemo y Galatea (1612), in royal octaves, which tells the love of Polyphemus, one of the Cyclopes, for the nymph Galatea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>'s Metamorphoses, and the Soledades (1613), written in a variety of metres. He also wrote sonnets, a poem of 79 royal octaves in praise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-gomez-de-sandoval-duke-of-lerma">Duke of Lerma</a> in 1617, the Fábula de Píramo y Tisbe of 1618, and plays.</p><p>The style called culteranismo or Gongorismo made heavy use of Latinate and Greek neologisms and of hyperbaton, a distortion of ordinary word order. Opponents coined the name from culto and luteranismo, to present it as a heresy of true poetry. Góngora revived or popularised many obscure words that are common today, such as brillante, asunto and fragmento. His lifelong rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-quevedo">Francisco de Quevedo</a>, associated with the witty, economical conceptismo, mocked his vocabulary and wrote satires on his nose and his gambling. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> counted him among the good poets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1626 a severe illness that seriously impaired his memory forced Góngora to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, where he died the following year, poor from his efforts to win positions and lawsuits for his relatives. He was buried in a side chapel of Córdoba Cathedral. Juan López de Vicuña published an edition in 1627, and an edition by Gonzalo de Hozes appeared in 1633.</p><p>The Generation of '27 took its name from 1927, when it marked the tercentenary of his death, which official academic circles had ignored, with recitals and a plan for a new critical edition. It was the first group to try to revive baroque literature. Dámaso Alonso called Góngora a mystic of words and argued against the idea that he had separate simple and difficult styles. Rafael Alberti and García Lorca wrote on him, and Velázquez painted his portrait.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luis de Góngora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…de Cervantes’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of Lope de Vega and Pedro Calderón de la Barca, the poetry of <b>Luis de Góngora</b> and Francisco de Quevedo, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose Las Meninas dates from 1656, Francisco de Zurbarán and Bartolomé Esteban Murillo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1603" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Luis de Góngora"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1603">What was here in 1603? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
